    bio: 'Dame Mary Douglas was a British social anthropologist born in San Remo, Italy, and educated at Oxford University, where she later returned to complete her doctoral work under E.E. Evans-Pritchard. After fieldwork among the Lele people of what is now the Democratic Republic of Congo, she developed a career at University College London that would produce some of the most philosophically fertile work in twentieth-century anthropology. Her intellectual range was extraordinary: she wrote with equal authority about the symbolism of food prohibitions in Leviticus, the sociology of risk, the anthropology of institutions, and the grid-group theory of cultural bias that she developed as a general framework for comparative cultural analysis.\n\nHer most celebrated work, Purity and Danger (1966), argued that pollution beliefs and taboo systems across cultures are not primitive attempts at hygiene or irrational superstition, but highly coherent symbolic systems for managing the anxiety produced by anomalies — things that fall between the established categories of a culture\'s classification scheme. Dirt, Douglas famously argued, is "matter out of place": not an objective physical property but a relational one, defined by its violation of a particular symbolic order. This insight had profound implications for the philosophy of culture, showing that what a society finds disgusting, dangerous, or sacred is always a reflection of the categories through which it organises reality — and that the figure who transgresses those categories carries a peculiar combination of danger and power.\n\nDouglas spent periods at various American universities, including Northwestern and Princeton, and was a deeply engaged public intellectual who applied her frameworks to contemporary political questions about risk perception, institutional life, and consumer culture. Her later work on the Book of Numbers and the literary structure of Leviticus showed that the same analytical tools she had applied to Lele cosmology could illuminate the deepest structures of the Biblical text. She was appointed a Dame Commander of the Order of the British Empire in 2006, the year before her death, in recognition of a body of work that permanently altered the relationship between anthropology, religious studies, and the philosophy of culture.',
